From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 16:06:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: fix multi-device calls, relay election, video
 orientation and reordered video

Found in live WhatsApp video calls between LuneOS phones and Android on
2026-11-03.

- Multi-device callee: the caller now sends each other rung device its
  own <terminate reason=accepted_elsewhere|rejected_elsewhere>, so the
  other devices stop ringing.
- A meowcaller callee sends <accept> only on the caller's first
  <mute_v2> after its user answered. Android callers send one, a
  meowcaller caller never did: the call stayed on the relay and the
  caller kept ringing. The caller now announces mute_v2 state 0 to the
  preaccepted devices every 1.5 s until answered, and the callee
  accepts anyway 3 s after its user answered.
- Relay election: the 1:1 relay is elected from both sides'
  <relaylatency>. Echoing the caller's numbers endorsed the caller's
  local relay, often one outside our relay block. An inbound call now
  steers to the is_fna relay, else the closest probed relay of our
  block we hold a token for, and reports that one near and all others
  far.
- RTCP: the reception report's WhatsApp extension carries the bytes
  received and the receive rate, as whatsapp-rust's native 1:1 compound
  packet does; left zero, a native sender lowers its video.
- Orientation: WhatsApp clients rotate by the low 2 bits of
  MediaFrameInfo in each video RTP packet. We stamp ours on every video
  packet, report the peer's (first frame and later changes) through
  OnVideoState and prefer them to the stanza's device_orientation.
  Every state=1 and state=6 we send carries the current orientation,
  also one set while the camera was off.
- Reordered video: the relay delivers video RTP out of order (seen up
  to 22 places late, none lost). The access unit assembler took every
  gap for a loss and withheld frames until an IDR, so the picture froze
  for the rest of the call. H264ReorderingAssembler puts packets back in
  order within a 32-packet window, per participant, and while frames
  are withheld the engine asks the peer for a keyframe every second.

Upstream's reception statistics (4603cff) and RTP orientation tracking
(2ca8d7d) are used where this patch used to carry its own.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
